% ============================================================================
% months — which number names each month of the year (adj-facts-stdlib, KINDERGARTEN).
% ============================================================================
% A second rung of the facts ladder, beside `shapes.adj`: the calendar fact a
% kindergartener chants — "January is the first month, December is the twelfth."
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? month_number(march, $Number)        % 3
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `month_number(month, number)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the month
% number WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not a month. The relation binds BOTH ways: forward
% `? month_number(may, $N)` gives 5, and reverse `? month_number($M, 5)` gives
% may. Because the `number` value is a NUMBER, a recalled month index composes
% straight into a formula (days-elapsed, quarter = ceil(number / 3)): the concrete
% bridge from kindergarten RECALL to kindergarten COMPUTE.
%
% Provenance: the month → number mapping is the ISO 8601 numbering, copied
% verbatim from Markus Kuhn's canonical ISO 8601 summary hosted by the University
% of Cambridge Computer Laboratory (an educational, .ac.uk reference for the
% international standard). The span at the `locator` states the endpoints of the
% numbering — "the month of the year between 01 (January) and 12 (December)" — and
% the twelve months run consecutively in the universal Gregorian order between
% those two anchors. `trust authoritative` — a primary reference for ISO 8601.
% Nothing here is asserted from memory. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table month_number {
    columns month, number

    row (january, 1)
    row (february, 2)
    row (march, 3)
    row (april, 4)
    row (may, 5)
    row (june, 6)
    row (july, 7)
    row (august, 8)
    row (september, 9)
    row (october, 10)
    row (november, 11)
    row (december, 12)

    source "MM is the month of the year between 01 (January) and 12 (December)"
    locator "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mgk25/iso-time.html"
    trust authoritative
}
